00:17:36.221 --> 00:17:39.258
make him the best person
to represent you in the U.S.

00:17:39.258 --> 00:17:39.525
Senate?

00:17:42.094 --> 00:17:44.863
Does that make him equipped

00:17:44.897 --> 00:17:48.500
to weigh in on the critical decisions

00:17:49.268 --> 00:17:52.204
about our economy
and our foreign policy and our future?

00:17:52.671 --> 00:17:55.474
But let let's do a thought experiment.

00:17:57.443 --> 00:17:59.711
Let's say you're at the airport

00:17:59.945 --> 00:18:01.847
and you see Mr.

00:18:01.847 --> 00:18:05.851
Walker and you say, hey,
there's Herschel Walker, Heisman winner.

00:18:06.151 --> 00:18:09.188
Let's have him fly the plane.

00:18:16.862 --> 00:18:19.698
You probably wouldn't say that.

00:18:20.132 --> 00:18:21.733
You'd want to know.

00:18:21.733 --> 00:18:24.803
Does he know how to fly an airplane?

00:18:26.472 --> 00:18:27.272
Or let's say

00:18:27.272 --> 00:18:30.542
you go to the hospital and and you sit.

00:18:30.676 --> 00:18:36.014
You say, you know that Walker guy,
he sure could turn up at Sanford Stadium,

00:18:36.748 --> 00:18:38.851
give him a scalpel

00:18:42.321 --> 00:18:45.023
No, you wouldn't say that.

00:18:45.023 --> 00:18:47.226
You'd ask at least outward.

00:18:48.060 --> 00:18:50.796
Has he done surgery before?

00:18:52.397 --> 00:18:54.766
And by the way, the opposite is true, too.

00:18:54.900 --> 00:18:59.104
Like, you may have liked me as president,
but you would not want me

00:18:59.404 --> 00:19:02.808
starting to tell that for the notes.

00:19:02.808 --> 00:19:07.179
I mean, can you imagine my slow, old,
skinny behind

00:19:07.179 --> 00:19:10.516
getting hit by some £300 defensive

00:19:10.516 --> 00:19:13.118
tackle who runs a 46 quarter?

00:19:13.986 --> 00:19:16.421
You'd have to scrape me off the field.

00:19:16.421 --> 00:19:17.222
No, I can't

00:19:20.058 --> 00:19:20.759
No, I can't.

00:19:20.759 --> 00:19:21.960
I'm good at a lot of things,

00:19:21.960 --> 00:19:24.229
but that would not be one of those things
that I'm good at.

00:19:30.269 --> 00:19:32.671
I mean, I can still hit a jump or not.

00:19:32.671 --> 00:19:35.307
But you wouldn't
want me on a football field

00:19:35.307 --> 00:19:37.776
running between the tackle and the gun.

00:19:37.776 --> 00:19:40.379
But my point is not

00:19:40.379 --> 00:19:44.583
that being a football player
disqualifies you from being a senator.

00:19:44.583 --> 00:19:47.186
If you had put in the work

00:19:48.687 --> 00:19:51.823
but in the case of Reverend

00:19:51.823 --> 00:19:54.626
Warnock's opponent,
there is very little evidence

00:19:55.194 --> 00:19:57.429
that he has taken any interest

00:19:57.829 --> 00:20:01.466
bother to learn anything
about or displayed

00:20:01.700 --> 00:20:07.172
any kind of inclination
towards public service or volunteer work.

00:20:07.506 --> 00:20:09.841
Or helping people in any way.

00:20:10.742 --> 00:20:12.945
At least we don't really know about it.

00:20:13.545 --> 00:20:16.515
And that does make you suspect

00:20:20.852 --> 00:20:22.588
Seems to me he's a

00:20:22.588 --> 00:20:24.756
celebrity who wants to be a politician.

00:20:26.658 --> 00:20:30.362
And we've seen how that goes.

00:20:36.268 --> 00:20:38.537
We've seen that before.

00:20:44.109 --> 00:20:46.044
And I'll make one more point

00:20:46.044 --> 00:20:48.680
as a general rule.

00:20:48.947 --> 00:20:52.217
I am not big on poking into people's
private lives

00:20:54.319 --> 00:20:55.954
I've always felt that that stuff is

00:20:55.954 --> 00:20:58.790
between a candidate and their family.

00:21:00.025 --> 00:21:02.060
But you know what's not between them

00:21:02.060 --> 00:21:03.495
and their family?

00:21:05.230 --> 00:21:07.399
Are issues of character

00:21:09.034 --> 00:21:11.403
being in the habit

00:21:11.403 --> 00:21:13.605
of not telling the truth.

00:21:14.973 --> 00:21:15.841
Being in the habit

00:21:15.841 --> 00:21:18.977
of saying one thing and doing another,

00:21:18.977 --> 00:21:21.580
being in the habit of having certain rules
for you

00:21:22.381 --> 00:21:25.217
and your important friends

00:21:26.618 --> 00:21:29.755
and other rules for everybody else

00:21:29.788 --> 00:21:31.323
that says something about

00:21:31.323 --> 00:21:34.459
the kind of leader you're going to be

00:21:34.459 --> 00:21:37.863
And if a candidate's main qualification
is that he's going to be loyal

00:21:37.863 --> 00:21:40.832
to Donald Trump,
it means that he's not really going to be

00:21:40.832 --> 00:21:44.836
thinking about you and your needs
and you deserve better.

00:21:45.337 --> 00:21:47.539
Georgia deserves better.

00:21:47.973 --> 00:21:50.776
You deserve somebody who's independent
and who's

00:21:50.776 --> 00:21:53.812
going to go work every day
and fight for you.

00:21:54.046 --> 00:21:59.284
Somebody like Brother Rafael Warnock,
who's been doing it all his life

00:21:59.651 --> 00:22:03.555
responsibly, conscientiously effectively,

00:22:03.989 --> 00:22:07.693
not just in the Senate,
but throughout his entire career,

00:22:09.661 --> 00:22:11.396
You deserve

00:22:11.897 --> 00:22:14.433
somebody who's also going to stand up

00:22:16.301 --> 00:22:18.603
for a woman's right to make her own health

00:22:18.603 --> 00:22:20.005
care decisions.

00:22:28.347 --> 00:22:28.947
Abortion

00:22:28.947 --> 00:22:32.551
is controversial in this country and

00:22:32.551 --> 00:22:34.753
I genuinely believe
there are people of good conscience

00:22:34.753 --> 00:22:38.657
who may differ from me on this issue.

00:22:39.391 --> 00:22:42.661
But we should all agree
that women everywhere

00:22:42.661 --> 00:22:44.629
should be able to control have a say in

00:22:44.629 --> 00:22:48.033
what happens with their own bodies.

00:22:48.133 --> 00:22:50.268
It shouldn't be controversial to say

00:22:50.502 --> 00:22:54.806
that the most personal of health care
choices should be made by a woman

00:22:54.806 --> 00:22:58.143
and her daughter, not by a bunch of mostly

00:22:58.143 --> 00:23:00.746
male politicians

00:23:04.049 --> 00:23:07.352
And that's why when the Supreme Court
struck down Roe versus Wade,

00:23:07.352 --> 00:23:11.256
it was a wake up call for a lot of people,
especially young women.

00:23:11.256 --> 00:23:13.859
Who may have taken Roe for granted.

00:23:14.593 --> 00:23:15.560
All right.

00:23:15.694 --> 00:23:18.764
It was a reminder
that a politicized court can reinterpret

00:23:19.464 --> 00:23:20.966
what we thought were well settled.

00:23:20.966 --> 00:23:22.100
Constitution no rights.

00:23:23.101 --> 00:23:23.502
Yeah.

00:23:23.602 --> 00:23:26.371
It's a reminder that history doesn't
just move forward.

00:23:26.371 --> 00:23:28.340
It can go backward to

00:23:30.876 --> 00:23:33.044
if Republicans take back the House
and the Senate.

00:23:33.478 --> 00:23:36.448
We could be one presidential election
away from a nationwide

00:23:36.448 --> 00:23:39.384
ban on access to abortion.

00:23:39.785 --> 00:23:42.320
And that might just be the beginning.

00:23:42.320 --> 00:23:44.589
I taught constitutional law
for over a decade.

00:23:44.589 --> 00:23:49.227
And I can tell you this if a court does
not believe in a zone of privacy

00:23:50.262 --> 00:23:52.764
that allows us to make certain decisions

00:23:52.764 --> 00:23:56.334
without government interference,

00:23:56.334 --> 00:23:57.836
then other freedoms

00:23:57.836 --> 00:24:00.439
we take for granted are also at risk.

00:24:00.439 --> 00:24:02.073
And I'm not just making this up.

00:24:02.073 --> 00:24:05.911
I'm not trying
to, you know, get everybody anxious.

00:24:06.278 --> 00:24:09.748
Justice
Thomas wrote as much in the Don's opinion.

00:24:10.081 --> 00:24:11.583
He said as much.

00:24:11.583 --> 00:24:15.153
If there is no right to privacy,
then same sex marriage could be addressed

00:24:16.721 --> 00:24:19.624
almost every
Republican in the House of Representatives

00:24:19.624 --> 00:24:24.729
has already voted against guaranteeing
a right to use contraception.

00:24:24.963 --> 00:24:28.266
The basis for that original decision
saying we had a right to

00:24:28.266 --> 00:24:30.535
contraception was a right to privacy,

00:24:31.536 --> 00:24:33.972
a zone
in which the government can interfere

00:24:35.807 --> 00:24:37.242
if they take power back.

00:24:37.242 --> 00:24:41.112
There's no guarantee that
that won't be next.

00:24:41.112 --> 00:24:42.514
So all of these issues

00:24:42.514 --> 00:24:44.549
are at stake in this election.

00:24:45.650 --> 00:24:48.653
And if that's not worth
15 minutes of your time,

00:24:50.255 --> 00:24:52.123
I don't know what is

00:24:54.426 --> 00:24:55.560
I don't know what it is.

00:24:55.560 --> 00:25:00.332
But just in case

00:25:01.766 --> 00:25:05.270
you need another reason to vote with me,

00:25:06.371 --> 00:25:10.275
consider the fact that democracy itself

00:25:10.275 --> 00:25:12.143
is also on the ballot

00:25:16.381 --> 00:25:17.749
I have to admit,

00:25:17.749 --> 00:25:21.753
sometimes going out on the campaign trail
feels a little harder than it used to

00:25:23.221 --> 00:25:27.125
And not just because I'm older and grayer,

00:25:28.493 --> 00:25:29.995
little stiffer

00:25:32.764 --> 00:25:35.700
Michelle says I'm still cute, though,

00:25:38.370 --> 00:25:43.408
But that back back

00:25:43.408 --> 00:25:45.777
when I back
when I was first running for office,

00:25:47.913 --> 00:25:49.981
people didn't agree with me on everything

00:25:52.017 --> 00:25:54.152
but we could at least talk to each other

00:25:55.754 --> 00:25:56.988
maybe once in a while.

00:25:56.988 --> 00:26:01.459
We could persuade each other
we could be civil towards each other.

00:26:03.361 --> 00:26:04.462
And that's why I ended up

00:26:04.462 --> 00:26:08.133
getting a whole bunch of Republican votes.

00:26:08.166 --> 00:26:09.568
And after I won,

00:26:09.568 --> 00:26:13.939
John McCain, my opponent, graciously
conceded, gave me a call publicly.

00:26:13.972 --> 00:26:16.007
Wish me luck for the sake of the country.

00:26:17.275 --> 00:26:19.711
And there was a peaceful transfer of power

00:26:20.679 --> 00:26:24.683
that basically foundation of our democracy

00:26:26.284 --> 00:26:29.054
is being called into question right now.

00:26:31.723 --> 00:26:33.158
Democrats aren't perfect.

00:26:33.158 --> 00:26:34.392
I'm the first one to admit it.

00:26:36.027 --> 00:26:38.229
Politicians, just like all of us,

00:26:38.229 --> 00:26:40.532
can make mistakes

00:26:41.833 --> 00:26:42.734
but right now,

00:26:42.734 --> 00:26:44.869
with a few notable exceptions,

00:26:47.405 --> 00:26:51.509
most of the GOP
and a whole bunch of these candidates

00:26:51.509 --> 00:26:54.412
are not even pretending
that the rules apply to them anymore.

00:26:55.513 --> 00:26:58.950
They are just making stuff up

00:26:59.684 --> 00:27:02.420
Late last year, Stacey's opponent

00:27:02.420 --> 00:27:04.856
signed one of the most aggressive voter
suppression

00:27:04.856 --> 00:27:06.291
laws in the country.

00:27:08.059 --> 00:27:11.997
And later on, he admitted he was, quote,

00:27:12.631 --> 00:27:18.136
I'm quoting here, frustrated
with the results of the 2020 election.

00:27:18.370 --> 00:27:22.173
So he, quote,
did something about it, unquote.

00:27:23.174 --> 00:27:23.708
Yeah.

00:27:25.010 --> 00:27:26.911
Now, let me tell you something.

00:27:26.911 --> 00:27:29.881
First time I ran for Congress,
I was already in the state legislature.

00:27:29.881 --> 00:27:31.650
I ran for Congress.

00:27:31.950 --> 00:27:33.551
I lost

00:27:34.452 --> 00:27:36.688
the primary by 30 points.

00:27:36.688 --> 00:27:37.922
Got what

00:27:42.060 --> 00:27:43.895
It was embarrassing.

00:27:44.162 --> 00:27:45.930
I had to go out the next day.

00:27:45.930 --> 00:27:48.166
I had a big L on my it

00:27:50.935 --> 00:27:53.038
And let
me tell you, I was frustrated, too.

00:27:54.839 --> 00:27:57.242
You know what I didn't do, though?

00:27:57.742 --> 00:27:59.878
I didn't claimed
that the election was rigged.

00:28:01.112 --> 00:28:03.915
I didn't try to stop votes
from being counted.

00:28:04.916 --> 00:28:07.519
I didn't inside a mob
to storm the Capitol.

00:28:08.787 --> 00:28:10.622
I took my lumps.

00:28:10.622 --> 00:28:14.192
I figured out why my campaign hadn't
connected to voters.

00:28:14.526 --> 00:28:17.095
I tried to run a better race the next time

00:28:18.797 --> 00:28:21.433
And by the way,
I won the next time because that's

00:28:21.433 --> 00:28:24.069
how the system works.

00:28:32.077 --> 00:28:32.744
Look,

00:28:33.945 --> 00:28:35.146
I get that.

00:28:35.146 --> 00:28:38.583
I get the democracy might not seem
like a top priority right now.

00:28:39.684 --> 00:28:43.455
One of the more depressing headlines
I saw was in a newspaper

00:28:43.655 --> 00:28:45.023
they've done a survey showed.

00:28:45.023 --> 00:28:49.094
75% of Americans
think that democracy is seriously at risk.

00:28:49.794 --> 00:28:51.062
But it's not their top priority.

00:28:53.498 --> 00:28:54.999
But I get that

00:28:56.568 --> 00:28:58.236
folks got bills to pay.

00:28:58.236 --> 00:29:00.638
They're worried about this.

00:29:00.672 --> 00:29:01.706
Keep on

00:29:02.273 --> 00:29:05.543
keeping on getting by

00:29:05.643 --> 00:29:07.779
and it doesn't always feel like

00:29:07.779 --> 00:29:11.850
the political process is working for you
when you don't see enough progress

00:29:11.850 --> 00:29:13.418
on the issues
that matter to you and your family.

00:29:13.418 --> 00:29:15.286
I understand this

00:29:16.354 --> 00:29:19.090
But we have seen throughout history.

00:29:19.591 --> 00:29:22.360
We see now around the world what happens

00:29:23.394 --> 00:29:26.197
if we give up on democracy.

00:29:26.831 --> 00:29:29.434
We can see in other countries
where the government tells you

00:29:29.434 --> 00:29:32.170
what books you can read and cannot read.

00:29:33.004 --> 00:29:36.975
Countries that put dissidents
and reporters in prison.

00:29:37.876 --> 00:29:40.111
Countries where it doesn't matter

00:29:40.111 --> 00:29:42.881
who you vote for, the people in power
will do whatever they want.

00:29:44.382 --> 00:29:46.484
Where corruption is rampant

00:29:46.484 --> 00:29:49.420
because there's no accountability

00:29:50.355 --> 00:29:52.223
When true democracy goes away.

00:29:53.825 --> 00:29:55.193
People get hurt.

00:29:55.193 --> 00:29:57.495
Yeah, it has real consequences.

00:29:58.963 --> 00:30:01.599
And that's why
generations of Americans have fought

00:30:02.667 --> 00:30:04.903
and some have died

00:30:04.903 --> 00:30:06.971
for the idea of self-government

00:30:07.839 --> 00:30:10.074
and set up rules to make it work.

00:30:10.975 --> 00:30:13.311
That's why folks right here in Georgia

00:30:13.845 --> 00:30:16.181
and all across this country fought.

00:30:16.648 --> 00:30:21.653
And some died to make sure that everybody
had the right to participate

00:30:22.253 --> 00:30:24.355
in this government.

00:30:29.961 --> 00:30:30.862
That's why

00:30:30.862 --> 00:30:32.130
that's why we teach our kids,

00:30:32.130 --> 00:30:35.433
even when they're little certain rules
about what's what's fair

00:30:35.900 --> 00:30:38.670
and what's honest, rules about how

00:30:38.670 --> 00:30:41.272
we should make decisions together
as a group.

00:30:41.806 --> 00:30:43.942
Everybody gets to say
everybody got to turn

00:30:44.809 --> 00:30:48.413
If you don't get your way,
don't throw a tantrum about it.

00:30:49.013 --> 00:30:51.950
Don't take your ball and go home.

00:30:51.950 --> 00:30:53.251
Get over it.

00:30:53.251 --> 00:30:54.586
Try to do better. In the next year.

00:30:57.956 --> 00:30:58.890
And that's what I mean when

00:30:58.890 --> 00:31:02.093
I say democracy's
at stake in this election.

00:31:03.695 --> 00:31:07.165
That's why it's not enough to elect
Democrats at the top of the ticket.

00:31:07.599 --> 00:31:10.168
We need to elect
good people up and down the ballot.

00:31:12.737 --> 00:31:13.805
Across the country.

00:31:13.805 --> 00:31:16.941
Some of the folks who tried to undermine
our democracy

00:31:16.941 --> 00:31:20.311
are running for office
that will oversee the next election.

00:31:20.478 --> 00:31:22.981
And if they win there's
no telling what might happen.

00:31:23.882 --> 00:31:26.217
And that's why we need to work
just as hard to elect

00:31:26.217 --> 00:31:28.286
secretaries of state like me when

00:31:29.721 --> 00:31:31.256
we got to fight for them.

00:31:31.256 --> 00:31:35.493
Just like we fight to elect
governors and senators who care about you,

00:31:36.594 --> 00:31:39.063
because if things get close,
they can make all the difference.

00:31:41.566 --> 00:31:43.001
And guess what?

00:31:44.302 --> 00:31:47.705
Now you get to make a difference to how

00:31:49.374 --> 00:31:50.842
you know, we joke in my house.

00:31:50.842 --> 00:31:53.645
Michelle, chill. Admit this.

00:31:53.912 --> 00:31:57.115
She can be a little glass half empty

00:31:57.115 --> 00:31:58.116
sometimes.

00:32:00.518 --> 00:32:03.955
And the hope and change got

00:32:04.422 --> 00:32:06.491
so I'm usually a little more optimistic

00:32:08.393 --> 00:32:11.663
But sometimes Michelle gets down
about the state of the country.

00:32:11.663 --> 00:32:13.464
In the state of the world,

00:32:15.033 --> 00:32:16.634
and I'll tell her

00:32:16.801 --> 00:32:19.370
everything will be okay.

00:32:20.338 --> 00:32:22.240
And I believe it will

00:32:23.808 --> 00:32:25.710
But I also know that things

00:32:25.710 --> 00:32:29.380
will not be okay on their own.

00:32:30.214 --> 00:32:33.284
We have to fight for this.

00:32:33.284 --> 00:32:35.687
Democracy is not self-executing.

00:32:35.954 --> 00:32:39.223
It depends on us.

00:32:39.223 --> 00:32:41.793
Working, nurturing,

00:32:43.027 --> 00:32:45.129
caring for it.

00:32:45.163 --> 00:32:47.899
Not just on Election Day,
but every day in between.

00:32:47.899 --> 00:32:51.436
It depends on us as citizens
saying This matters.

00:32:53.137 --> 00:32:54.605
This election matters.

00:32:54.605 --> 00:32:55.707
Georgia

00:32:57.041 --> 00:32:58.810
These are tough times.

00:32:58.810 --> 00:33:01.913
The good news, though, is
we've been through tough times before.

00:33:04.482 --> 00:33:05.116
The important

00:33:05.116 --> 00:33:08.086
thing is to resist the temptation
to give up.

00:33:08.987 --> 00:33:10.788
To turn inward.

00:33:10.788 --> 00:33:13.291
To see politics as a means zero sum

00:33:13.291 --> 00:33:16.661
game where anything goes
and rules are made to be broken.

00:33:17.061 --> 00:33:20.198
And the only way for people like us to win

00:33:20.398 --> 00:33:22.567
is if people like them lose

00:33:25.837 --> 00:33:28.373
I don't I don't believe in those politics

00:33:29.574 --> 00:33:31.743
because even in our darkest moments,

00:33:32.543 --> 00:33:35.380
and there have been darker moments
in this country before,

00:33:37.281 --> 00:33:39.951
we have always had

00:33:39.951 --> 00:33:42.553
more in common than our politics.

00:33:42.553 --> 00:33:43.321
Suggest

00:33:44.956 --> 00:33:46.624
Even when times are tough.

00:33:46.624 --> 00:33:49.327
What unites us

00:33:49.894 --> 00:33:51.929
has always been stronger
than what divides us.

00:33:53.598 --> 00:33:57.301
There have always been certain values
that bind us together as citizens,

00:33:57.502 --> 00:34:02.073
no matter who we are, where we come from,
what we look like or who we love.

00:34:02.240 --> 00:34:04.375
That's the promise of America.

